WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
When hearing fails, which hidden step broke?

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:09.020
Sound enters the ear canal, the coiled cochlea turns it into nerve signals, and

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.360
those reach the brainstem.

4
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.820
Surprisingly, a healthy cochlea even sends faint sound back out into the ear canal.

5
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.640
To test hearing, scientists read signals at separate stages along that chain.

6
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:30.703
Start at the ear canal: a sealed microphone records faint sound the cochlea makes.

7
00:00:31.903 --> 00:00:35.523
This otoacoustic emission screens newborns for hearing loss.

8
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:40.983
Inside, sound sets up a travelling wave along the basilar membrane.

9
00:00:42.343 --> 00:00:46.223
Each place moves most for one frequency, high ones near the base.

10
00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:52.083
Scientists measure one place as a tone gets louder, alive and after death.

11
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:58.847
Membrane motion pushes each hair bundle, a staircase of stereocilia linked by tip

12
00:00:58.847 --> 00:01:04.767
links. Holding the cell at a negative voltage, scientists push the bundle toward its

13
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:10.827
tallest row. Tip links are thought to pull channels open, so positive ions flow in.

14
00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:17.407
More push opens more channels, so peak current traces an S-shaped curve until nearly

15
00:01:17.407 --> 00:01:18.407
all are open.

16
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.400
Next, inner hair cells pass signals to nerve fibres at contacts called ribbon

17
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.360
synapses. Threshold tests then find the quietest sound that evokes a response.

18
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.520
An audiogram plots behavioural thresholds by frequency; electrical tests record from

19
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.760
the nerve and brainstem.

20
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.160
So different tests read different stages, from ear canal to brainstem.

21
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.613
Now test that idea.

22
00:01:48.253 --> 00:01:52.853
Suppose a mouse faces loud noise, yet later passes a standard hearing threshold

23
00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:58.593
test. Does a normal threshold prove its ribbon synapses escaped damage?

24
00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.573
Pause and decide what that passing test can, and cannot, tell you.

25
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:13.933
No. A mouse can pass its hearing test after loud noise.

26
00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:19.193
Yet it can lose about half the ribbon synapses at one cochlear place.

27
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:24.953
A threshold only tests the quietest response, so one pass can mislead.

28
00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:31.373
Comparing each cochlea alive and after death separates what living cells add from

29
00:02:31.373 --> 00:02:32.373
the bare wave.

30
00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:35.783
Together, these methods unlocked three findings.

31
00:02:37.163 --> 00:02:42.363
First, a dead cochlea still carries the wave, but weakly, broadly tuned, and its

32
00:02:42.363 --> 00:02:46.563
peak can shift. So the living cochlea adds amplification.

33
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:52.263
It comes from three rows of outer hair cells, which shorten and lengthen with

34
00:02:52.263 --> 00:02:56.603
voltage. Their side-wall protein prestin is the motor.

35
00:02:57.667 --> 00:03:03.207
Second, turtle hair-cell channels switch on with a time constant, a speed measure,

36
00:03:03.607 --> 00:03:05.587
as short as about 50 microseconds.

37
00:03:06.867 --> 00:03:11.447
That is too fast for a chain of enzymes, so force is thought to open channels

38
00:03:11.447 --> 00:03:17.907
directly. Third, in mice, loud noise can cost about half the ribbon synapses at one

39
00:03:17.907 --> 00:03:20.247
place, while thresholds stay normal.

40
00:03:21.507 --> 00:03:24.327
So testing several stages shows where hearing broke.
